# How Git is allowed to touch these files on the way in and out of the repository.
#
# This exists because of a real failure. The first push to GitHub turned CI red on two tests
# that pass on every developer machine: the working copy held bare LF, the Windows runners
# check out with core.autocrlf=true, and the installer script therefore arrived as CRLF. A
# multiline `$` in .NET matches before \n and not before \r\n, so an anchored search for the
# [UninstallRun] section silently found nothing in a file that plainly had one.
#
# Two things were wrong and both are fixed. The tests no longer care - Repo.Read normalises
# line endings, because a test asserting what a file SAYS should not change its answer with a
# checkout setting. And the repository now says what it wants, so a checkout is the same
# everywhere instead of depending on each contributor's Git configuration.

# Normalise everything text to LF in the repository. Working copies still get the platform's
# own endings on checkout, which is what Windows tools expect.
* text=auto

# The files where the ending is part of the contract, or where a tool is fussy.
*.cs        text diff=csharp
*.csproj    text
*.props     text
*.sln       text eol=crlf
*.yml       text eol=lf
*.yaml      text eol=lf
*.ps1       text eol=crlf
*.iss       text eol=crlf
*.md        text
*.json      text
*.txt       text

# Binary, and it matters. Quicksand must reach the assembly byte for byte: OFL condition 3
# makes "Quicksand" a Reserved Font Name, so a modified file may not keep the name, and a
# line-ending conversion is a modification. The screenshots are the README's evidence that
# every image is the product rather than a mockup, and a converted PNG is a corrupt one.
*.ttf       binary
*.otf       binary
*.png       binary
*.jpg       binary
*.ico       binary
*.bin       binary
*.onnx      binary
*.spm       binary
*.db        binary
*.exe       binary
*.dll       binary

# Keep generated evidence out of the language statistics: the shots are pictures of the
# product, not source, and the plans are working documents rather than shipped code.
docs/shots/**            linguist-generated=true
docs/superpowers/**      linguist-documentation=true
